trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Caregiver lacks recall of maternal exposures and NICU admission status — common in early postpartum settings. Since objective clinical data is now needed to advance the diagnosis, I must proceed to targeted physical examination findings. In neonatal respiratory distress, critical exam elements include: heart rate and rhythm (tachycardia, bradycardia, murmurs), presence/quality of peripheral pulses (weak or absent femorals suggest coarctation), precordial activity (hyperdynamic vs. quiet), lung auscultation (crackles, decreased air entry, wheezes), and assessment for signs of congenital anomalies (e.g., dysmorphic features, abdominal distension, scaphoid abdomen suggesting diaphragmatic hernia). These are essential before ordering labs/imaging.

[TEST] Perform a targeted physical examination including:  
- Heart auscultation for murmurs, gallops, or abnormal splitting;  
- Assessment of peripheral pulses (especially femoral) and capillary refill time;  
- Lung auscultation for breath sounds, crackles, or asymmetry;  
- Inspection for dysmorphic features, chest wall deformities, or scaphoid abdomen.

